{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 320,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thanks to Google for sponsoring a portion of \nthis video, for supporting small businesses,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4160,
    "dDurationMs": 2960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and for helping people find \njobs. More about that at the end."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10640,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Atomic orbitals have long been a source of \nfrustration for me. On the one hand, you  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 14800,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have simple cartoon diagrams that make them feel \nfriendly... but which are so varied and vague they  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 19680,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "don’t really convey much beyond the basic idea \nthat atoms have a nucleus and some electrons. Some  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 24400,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "people try to take the cartoony diagrams and make \nthem feel more random, or “quantum” – which is an  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29040,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "improvement – but they’re still very much just \ncartoon stand-ins for the vague idea of “atom”."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34560,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On the other hand, you have atomic orbitals \ndepicted as fuzzy clouds or balloons or  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39120,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rainbow donuts, which are definitely more \ntechnically accurate (or technically inspired),  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44240,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but none of which feel like they give me a sense \nof what’s actually going on - like, what does this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48800,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "blobby thing have to do with orbiting particles? \nIs the electron inside it? Or on the surface? Why  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 54400,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "do some of them have more blobs and others donuts? \nAnd why are some of the donuts rainbow-colored?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 59680,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I want to know what an atom looks like! And I \nwant that picture to actually have something  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 63440,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to do with the nitty gritty reality of atoms \n(since they are, indeed, real things). You know,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 68320,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like how a diagram of the solar system is both \na totally-not-to-scale caricature and yet also  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73360,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "represents the very real idea that the planets \nall orbit the sun in roughly the same plane,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 77760,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and that some are farther out and some \nare closer in; and if it’s animated we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 80960,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "get to see that the closer planets complete \ntheir orbits more often. It’s a nice picture!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85360,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that’s what I want for atoms: a good picture.\nThere are a few things I’d like that picture to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 90160,
    "dDurationMs": 2480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "get across, some of them because \nthey’re important for the physics  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 92640,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of atoms, and some of them because they’re \nquestions my brain wants answers to. Like: "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96960,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Where is the electron?\nHow fast is it orbiting? "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 99840,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "How much energy does it have?\nHow big is the picture relative to other pictures?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 104320,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Of course, the wave-particle nature of quantum \nmechanics means some of these ideas (like the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 108400,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electron’s position) don’t translate exactly from \nour everyday intuition to the atomic scale...  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113280,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "BUT, there is a way of thinking about \nwave-particle duality where you picture  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 116800,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the wavefunction as a bunch of water, and the \nparticle as a speck of dust in that water;  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 120960,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the particle is mostly guided by where the water \ngoes (and the water is guided by the equations  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 125280,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that determine how water behaves depending \non its circumstances). And, if you apply the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 129040,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mathematics of that idea to atomic orbitals, \nand then render it in 3D, here’s what you get."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 136720,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Isn’t it beautiful? Here’s another. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 151840,
    "dDurationMs": 560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And another."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 154800,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact, I made a bunch of these. And \nthey’re all mesmerizing, and beautiful,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 159440,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and isn’t the ground state of the hydrogen atom \njust so cute?? And aren't the excited states so  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 164320,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "majestic? There’s so much structure and detail in \nthem, I love it. You can see the patterns in the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 170320,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "orbitals, you can get a sense that they actually \nARE orbitals (I mean, something is orbiting!)."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 175600,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ok, so I do have to be clear: the dots don’t \neach represent a separate electron - the whole  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 180880,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "collection represents the wavefunction of a single \nelectron, and the individual dots represent all  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 185360,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the places that electron could be (a higher \ndensity of dots means a higher probability of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 189600,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the electron being there). Electrons with more \nenergy are more likely to be far away from the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 196400,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nucleus, so higher energy orbitals are bigger. \nThe motion of the dots is showing the “flow” of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199600,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the wavefunction, and DOES correspond to an extent \nwith its actual angular momentum, though they’re  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 204960,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "NOT electron trajectories. Unless you think \nBohmian trajectories are real (in which case,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 209280,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they really are electron trajectories!). I’ll let \nthe philosophers of physics fight that one out."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 213600,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the point is these visuals are created by \nrepresenting actual electron wavefunctions in a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 218000,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "visual language that our brains can understand - \nthat of objects and light and shadows and motion  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 222960,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in 3D space. There’s actually stuff orbiting!! \nAnd they’re pretty! I hope you like them too."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 229840,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Oh, one final thing - I 100% get that these \nare not easy to draw. So if you want a cartoon  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 235360,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "representation of an atom that’s simple \nbut more closely based in atomic physics,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 239040,
    "dDurationMs": 3040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "here’s my proposal: it’s based on the three \n“P” orbitals (the ones from the “P” block of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242080,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the periodic table): one of them has the electron \norbiting one way, one in the opposite way, and in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 247040,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the third one the electron is orbiting the same \namount but around some perpendicular direction,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 250960,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and we can’t know which, which is why the points \naren't moving in the middle orbital, and why I’ve  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 255040,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "drawn a dotted line and question mark for the \nsideways circle. And if you want you can add  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 258720,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an electron to each orbital - or two electrons, \nif one is oriented spin up and one spin down.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 263600,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "THIS is a MinutePhysics-approved \ncartoon representation of an atom."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 274400,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thanks to Google for sponsoring this portion \nof my video. When Google reached out to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 278000,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sponsor MinutePhysics, I immediately \nsaid yes, because I use Google search  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 281680,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "literally every day for everything from 3D \nsoftware tutorials, to physics equations,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 285680,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the weather, recipes, and of course coronavirus \nvaccine information. And Google has been focusing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 290560,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a lot on supporting individuals and small \nbusinesses over the last year - Google can  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 294560,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even help *you* or your family and friends \ndiscover opportunities and find jobs where  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 298560,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you live! Just search \"jobs for physics \nmajors\" or \"jobs for veterans in Montana\"  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 303360,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or \"jobs for\" whatever it is you do, and Google \nwill help you find opportunities tailored to you!  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 308880,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thanks again to Google for sponsoring \nthis part of my video, for making it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 312000,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "easy to convert centimeters to inches, and for \nhelping us all find the information we need."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 317600,
    "dDurationMs": 2560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ok, the sponsored segment is \nover and you're still here!  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 321040,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You probably want to know about the \nrainbow donuts. The color there represents  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 324400,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the \"phase\" of the wavefunction, which informs \nhow different wavefunctions interfere with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 330080,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "each other, and it's essentially being \nrepresented by motion in my 3D visuals."
    } ]
  } ]
}
